BEGIN:VEVENT
IMAGE;VALUE=URI;DISPLAY=BADGE;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://inprinthouston.org
 /wp-content/uploads/2026/07/Andrea-Bajani-and-Claire-Vaye-Watkins-closeup-
 1-for-website.jpg
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://inprinthouston.org/wp-content/uploads/202
 6/07/Andrea-Bajani-and-Claire-Vaye-Watkins-closeup-1-for-website.jpg
UID:OH76Xv6yj7LFCjYux2twy
SUMMARY:Inprint Andrea Bajani & Claire Vaye Watkins Reading
DTSTAMP:20260806T110908Z
DTSTART:20260824T193000Z
DESCRIPTION:General admission tickets will go on sale on Monday\, August 3\
	, 2026 at 12 pm. \nSeason tickets are on sale here. \nAndrea Bajani and Cl
	aire Vaye Watkins will read from their new novels\, respectively\, The Ann
	iversary (translated by Geoffrey Brock) and Yellow Pine\, followed by an o
	n-stage conversation led by professor and National Book Critics Circle Awa
	rd finalist Lacy M. Johnson. The evening will conclude with a book sale an
	d signing. The event is presented as part of the 2026/2027 Inprint Margare
	tt Root Brown Reading Series.  \nAndrea Bajani is one of the most recogniz
	ed and lauded authors of contemporary Italian and European literature. His
	 many novels include If You Kept a Record of Sins\, called “astonishing” b
	y The New York Times\, and Every Promise\, which won the Bagutta Prize\, t
	he oldest Italian literary prize. A self-described existential writer\, he
	 often examines the personal\, philosophical\, and political\, while confr
	onting the unanswerable. A professor and international writer-in-residence
	 at Rice University\, Bajani joins us with his latest novel\, The Annivers
	ary\, which won the 2025 Strega Prize\, one of Europe’s major literary awa
	rds. Currently being translated into more than 25 languages\, The Annivers
	ary is a subtle yet heartrending portrait of an abusive marriage in patria
	rchal Italy and a son’s liberating decision to cut contact with his parent
	s. The Times Literary Supplement writes\, “Bajani’s prose is sharp and bri
	ms with psychological insights . . . Perhaps its most salient feature—in d
	eft translation by Geoffrey Brock—is its skill at depicting alienation . .
	 . Like Albert Camus\, Bajani is a poet of estrangement.” \nClaire Vaye Wa
	tkins has been called “the most riveting voice of the unsalvageable West” 
	(Los Angeles Times). Her debut novel Gold Fame Citrus\, inspired by the Ca
	lifornian drought and by the lives of outcasts in the desert\, blends clim
	ate fiction with environmental history. Born in Bishop\, California and ra
	ised in the Mojave Desert on the Amargosa River\, Watkins is also the auth
	or of the novel I Love You But I’ve Chosen Darkness and the short story co
	llection Battleborn\, winner of the Story Prize\, the Dylan Thomas Prize\,
	 and the New York Public Library’s Young Lions Fiction Award. A National B
	ook Foundation “5 Under 35” honoree and a Guggenheim Fellow\, Watkins come
	s to Houston with her newest work Yellow Pine\, described by author Megha 
	Majumdar as “an exhilarating novel about a woman in the Mojave desert who 
	wants to be a mother one more time\, wants to love freely and arrange her 
	life freely\, wants to live outside the maze of money and ads and being so
	ld to\, a novel with huge and electric convictions.” \nLacy M. Johnson is 
	a Houston-based professor\, curator\, and activist\, and is author of The 
	Reckonings and The Other Side — both National Book Critics Circle Award fi
	nalists — and Trespasses. She is editor\, with the designer Cheryl Beckett
	\, of More City Than Water: A Houston Flood Atlas. She teaches creative no
	nfiction at Rice University\, where she is the Director of the MFA in Crea
	tive Writing Program\, and is also the Founding Director of the Houston Fl
	ood Museum. \n\n\nhttps://inprinthouston.org/event/inprint-andrea-bajani-c
	laire-vaye-watkins-reading/\n\nFrom Inprint Houston
URL:https://inprinthouston.org/event/inprint-andrea-bajani-claire-vaye-watk
	ins-reading/
LOCATION:Congregation Emanu El\, 1500 Sunset Boulevard\, Houston\, TX\, 770
	05
X-CALRIPPER-SOURCE:inprint-houston
CATEGORIES:Inprint Houston
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
DURATION:PT2H
END:VEVENT
